## Proof

**Setup.** Let $D$ be the diagonal operator on $\ell^2(\mathbb{N})$ associated to the sequence $(a_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}$, i.e. the operator defined on the standard orthonormal basis $(e_n)$ by
$$De_n = a_n e_n, \qquad n\in\mathbb{N},$$
and extended linearly (and continuously, when bounded) to $\ell^2(\mathbb{N})$.

The forward implication is classical: if $(a_n)\in\ell^\infty(\mathbb{N})$, then $D$ is bounded with $\|D\|=\sup_n|a_n|$.

We prove the **converse**:

> **Claim.** If $D\in L(\ell^2(\mathbb{N}))$ (i.e. $D$ is a bounded linear operator on $\ell^2(\mathbb{N})$), then $(a_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}\in\ell^\infty(\mathbb{N})$.

**Proof of the claim.** Assume $D\in L(\ell^2(\mathbb{N}))$, and let $\|D\|$ denote its operator norm:
$$\|D\| = \sup_{\|x\|_2=1}\|Dx\|_2 < \infty.$$

For each $n\in\mathbb{N}$, the standard basis vector $e_n$ belongs to $\ell^2(\mathbb{N})$ and satisfies $\|e_n\|_2 = 1$. By definition of $D$,
$$De_n = a_n e_n,$$
so
$$\|De_n\|_2 = \|a_n e_n\|_2 = |a_n|\,\|e_n\|_2 = |a_n|.$$

On the other hand, boundedness of $D$ gives
$$\|De_n\|_2 \leq \|D\|\,\|e_n\|_2 = \|D\|.$$

Combining the two displayed equalities/inequalities,
$$|a_n| = \|De_n\|_2 \leq \|D\| \qquad\text{for every } n\in\mathbb{N}.$$

Taking the supremum over $n$,
$$\sup_{n\in\mathbb{N}} |a_n| \leq \|D\| < \infty.$$

Hence $(a_n)_{n\in\mathbb{N}}\in\ell^\infty(\mathbb{N})$. $\blacksquare$

**Remark (full characterization).** Combining the forward direction with the converse, and noting that the argument above also yields $\sup_n|a_n|\leq\|D\|$ while the forward direction yields $\|D\|\leq\sup_n|a_n|$, we obtain the complete equivalence
$$D\in L(\ell^2(\mathbb{N})) \iff (a_n)\in\ell^\infty(\mathbb{N}),$$
with the exact norm formula
$$\|D\| = \sup_{n\in\mathbb{N}}|a_n|.$$

